@*
    OdsSecretClearDialog — the confirmation in front of clearing a stored secret
    (Odyssey Design System · components/SecretClearDialog).

    ONE DIALOG, TWO COPY VARIANTS, chosen by kind — because the two kinds carry different losses and
    a single wording would either overstate one or understate the other.

      RotatableCredential  the value is gone from the store and the feature stops until a new one is
                           entered. Recoverable: re-issue it at the provider and paste it back.
      DerivationKey        the same, plus the part that cannot be walked back — everything already
                           derived with this key stops being re-derivable, for good. There is no
                           provider to re-issue it from.

    The irreversible clause is a coral callout rather than the amber advisory band used elsewhere:
    an advisory is about a cost the administrator chose, and this is the loss itself.

    ONE CONFIRM BUTTON, not a typed confirmation. The action is already two clicks behind Clear, the
    dialog states the consequence in its own words, and a value that cannot be read back cannot be
    re-typed to prove intent — transcription would only be theatre.

    Built on OdsFormDialog because Components/ contains no confirmation dialog to reuse, and the form
    shell already owns the focus trap, the Esc/scrim dismissal and the busy state on the submit
    button — a second dialog pattern would have to re-earn all three.

    DEVIATION FROM THE DESIGN SYSTEM, deliberately: the DS reaches for this from the caller rather
    than from the field, reasoning that "the copy stays with the caller that knows the key". In this
    implementation the FIELD knows the key, so that rationale does not transfer — and keeping the
    dialog with the field is what lets a failed Clear render inside the still-open dialog rather than
    behind its scrim (PR #450 frontend review).
*@

<OdsFormDialog Open="Open"
               OpenChanged="OpenChanged"
               Icon="@(IsDerivationKey ? "key_off" : "delete_outline")"
               IconTone="OdsModalTone.Error"
               Title="@($"Clear {Title}?")"
               Subtitle="This cannot be undone from here."
               CancelLabel="Cancel"
               SubmitLabel="Clear value"
               SubmitIcon="delete"
               SubmitVariant="OdsButtonVariant.Danger"
               OnSubmit="OnConfirm">
    @* The settings key, monospaced. Two credentials in one group can have similar titles, and this is
       the unambiguous identifier of the row being cleared. The chip's meaning is carried visually by
       its monospace treatment, which a screen reader cannot convey, so the label is supplied as text
       (WCAG 1.3.1). *@
    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(SecretKey))
    {
        <div class="odc-secret-dialog-key">
            <span class="sr-only">Setting key: </span>@SecretKey
        </div>
    }

    <p class="odc-secret-dialog-p">
        The stored value is deleted from the settings store. Nothing recovers it from a database
        backup — the ciphertext is unreadable without the encryption key ring.
    </p>

    @if (Unreadable)
    {
        <p class="odc-secret-dialog-p">
            This row is already <b>unreadable</b>, so clearing it changes nothing that is currently
            working. It removes the failing row so a new value can be entered — but if the original
            Data Protection key ring can still be restored, that would recover the value and clearing
            it now will not.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p class="odc-secret-dialog-p">
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Consequence))
            {
                @Consequence
                <text> </text>
            }
            The row returns to <b>not set</b>, and behaves as it did before any value was configured.
        </p>
    }

    @if (IsDerivationKey)
    {
        <div class="odc-secret-irrev">
            <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true">report</span>
            <div>
                <b>This key cannot be re-issued.</b> Anything already derived from it becomes
                permanently un-re-derivable — clearing it cannot be walked back, and entering a new
                value does not restore the old relationships. Export the current value first if you
                have any way to.
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    @* A failed Clear leaves this dialog OPEN — OdsFormDialog closes only when OnSubmit returns true —
       so a message rendered only in the field below sits behind the scrim and is invisible to a
       sighted user (PR #450 frontend review). The failure has to render where focus already is. No
       role="alert": the announcement travels through the field's OnAnnounce to the page's live
       region, and the field's own copy carries the role once the dialog is dismissed. *@
    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Error))
    {
        <div class="odc-secret-error odc-secret-confirm-error">
            <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true">error_outline</span>
            <span>@Error</span>
        </div>
    }
</OdsFormDialog>

@code {
    [Parameter] public bool Open { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> OpenChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The credential's human name, used in the dialog title.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The registry key, shown as the unambiguous identifier of the row being cleared.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? SecretKey { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Selects the copy variant — the one thing the recoverability classification drives.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool IsDerivationKey { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the row being cleared is already undecryptable, which changes what is lost.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Unreadable { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What stops working once the row returns to unset — the same sentence the advisory carries.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Consequence { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A failed clear's message, rendered inside the dialog rather than behind its scrim.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Error { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Returns <c>true</c> to close. Returning <c>false</c> keeps the dialog open on failure.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<Task<bool>>? OnConfirm { get; set; }
}
